<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1029</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral is the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, the ancient capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, standing on the site of the first church built after the conversion of King Mirian III in the fourth century. The present building was completed around 1029 by the architect Arsukisdze, and it was long the coronation and burial church of the Georgian kings. It is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svetitskhoveli-cathedral.jpg" alt="Svetitskhoveli Cathedral" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ... · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetitskhoveli_Cathedral_09.23_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Georgian hagiography, a Georgian Jew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> named Elias was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was crucified, bought Jesus' robe from a Roman soldier and brought it home. His sister Sidonia died on touching it and was buried with it. A cedar grew from her grave, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nino">Saint Nino</a> had seven columns made from it for the church. The seventh rose into the air until she prayed through the night, and a healing liquid was said to flow from it. In Georgian sveti means pillar and tskhoveli means life-giving, hence the name.</p><p>The first church was built in the fourth century in the reign of Mirian III of Kartli (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>), at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Mtkvari</a> and Aragvi rivers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> officially adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as its state religion in 337. King Vakhtang Gorgasali later built a basilica in the late fifth century, whose base was found in the restoration of 1970–71, along with the remains of an even older wooden church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The present building was constructed between 1010 and 1029 by the architect Arsukidze, at the invitation of Catholicos Melchizedek I, in the reign of King Giorgi I. It follows the cross-dome plan that became the principal Georgian style after the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> under Bagrat III. A legend, for which there is no documentary evidence, says that the architect's right hand was cut off out of jealousy; a relief of a hand holding a square and an inscription on the east facade name him.</p><p>The cathedral was damaged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and during Russian and Soviet rule, and by earthquakes. It was rebuilt at the end of the fourteenth century after Tamerlane destroyed it, the present dome dates from the fifteenth century, and renovations followed in the mid-seventeenth century. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> had the defensive wall built in 1787. In the early 1830s, before a planned visit by Emperor Nicholas I, Russian authorities demolished the galleries and whitewashed the frescoes, though the tsar never came.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Svetitskhoveli was the site of the coronation of Georgian kings and their burial place. Ten are known to have been buried there, though only six tombs have been found, among them those of Vakhtang Gorgasali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> and George XII, the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. A small chapel built into the south side is a symbolic copy of the Chapel of the Holy Sepulchre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, marking the church as the second most sacred place after Jerusalem because of Christ's robe. Some frescoes survive after restoration, including a thirteenth-century Beast of the Apocalypse. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> report of 2010 found structural issues threatening the cathedral's stability.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Svetitskhoveli Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rock-cut monastery of Vardzia, consecrated in the 1180s, the Bagrati Cathedral at Kutaisi and the rebuilt <b>Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</b> at Mtskheta, finished around 1029, show the ambition of Georgian church architecture, while goldsmiths and fresco painters produced works of high quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</b> at Mtskheta stands on the site of the first royal church and remains one of the principal cathedrals of the Georgian Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
